"""King Arthur and the Round Table: a VenvLearning retelling for Grade 4. Chapters 18–22."""
from readers.arthur_a import ch

ch("Sir Meliagraunce",
   [("kidnap", "to take someone away by force"), ("cart", "a wooden wagon pulled by a horse"),
    ("ambush", "a surprise attack from a hiding place"), ("disgrace", "shame; loss of respect")],
   ["Every year on the first day of May, Queen Guenevere liked to go “a-Maying.” She and her ladies and ten knights dressed in green rode into the fields and woods to gather flowers and celebrate spring. Because it was a peaceful holiday, the knights wore no armor and carried only light weapons.",
    "A knight named Sir Meliagraunce had long admired the queen. He knew she would be out in the fields with only ten knights, all unarmed. He hid in the woods with one hundred soldiers in full armor. When the queen's party passed by, Meliagraunce and his men sprang out in an ambush.",
    "The queen's ten knights fought bravely, even without armor, but they were outnumbered. Several were wounded. To save their lives, the queen agreed to go with Meliagraunce to his castle. Meliagraunce had kidnapped the queen. Before she left, Guenevere secretly gave a ring to a young page and whispered, “Ride to Sir Lancelot. Tell him what has happened.”",
    "Meliagraunce knew that Lancelot would come, so he hid thirty archers along the road. When Lancelot came galloping to the rescue, the archers shot his horse. Lancelot was left on foot in heavy armor, still far from the castle.",
    "Just then, a woodcutter came by with a cart. Lancelot asked to ride in it. In those days, riding in a cart was a disgrace. Carts were used to carry criminals to their punishment, and no knight would ever be seen riding in one. But Lancelot did not care what people thought. All that mattered was saving the queen. He climbed into the cart and made the driver hurry to the castle.",
    "When people saw the great Lancelot riding in a cart, they laughed and pointed. But Lancelot banged on the castle gate and shouted, “Meliagraunce, you traitor! Come out and fight!”",
    "Meliagraunce was terrified. He ran to the queen and begged for mercy. Guenevere, wanting to avoid more bloodshed, agreed to make peace. She calmed Lancelot down, and the wounded knights were cared for. But Meliagraunce had not changed. He still planned to cause trouble.",
    "Soon after, Meliagraunce tricked Lancelot into falling through a trapdoor into a dungeon. But a kind lady of the castle helped Lancelot escape. Meanwhile, Meliagraunce went to King Arthur and accused the queen of wrongdoing. Once again, there would be a trial by combat.",
    "On the day of the trial, Meliagraunce expected that Lancelot would still be locked in the dungeon and could not come. But Lancelot rode onto the field just in time. The two knights fought, and Lancelot struck Meliagraunce down. The queen was cleared.",
    "Once again, Lancelot had saved Queen Guenevere. He had been willing to give up his pride, and even his good name, by riding in the cart. But the bond between Lancelot and the queen was growing stronger, and more and more people at court were beginning to notice."])

ch("The Civil War",
   [("civil war", "a war between people of the same country"), ("rescue", "to save from danger"),
    ("vengeance", "punishment given to get even for a wrong"), ("siege", "surrounding a castle to force it to give up")],
   ["King Arthur had many nephews. Two of them, Sir Agravaine and Sir Mordred, were jealous of Lancelot. They wanted to bring him down. They knew that Lancelot loved the queen more than a knight should, so they went to King Arthur and told him.",
    "Arthur's oldest nephew, Gawaine, was angry with his brothers. “Do not do this,” he said. “Lancelot has saved the king and queen many times. If you start this quarrel, it will destroy the Round Table.” His younger brothers Gareth and Gaheris agreed with Gawaine. But Agravaine and Mordred would not listen.",
    "King Arthur was deeply troubled. He loved Lancelot like a brother, and he did not want to believe them. But he was the king, and the law said that he had to find out the truth. Agravaine and Mordred set a trap. They caught Lancelot visiting the queen's room. Lancelot fought his way out and escaped, but Agravaine was killed in the fight. Mordred ran to tell the king.",
    "By the law of the land, the queen was sentenced to be burned. Arthur's heart was breaking, but he felt he had to obey the law, or no one would trust his justice again. He ordered his knights to guard the queen at the fire. Gawaine refused to be there, but his younger brothers Gareth and Gaheris obeyed the king. They came without armor or weapons, to show that they did not want to fight.",
    "Lancelot and his friends rode to the rescue. They charged into the crowd, swinging their swords. In the rush and confusion, Lancelot struck down Gareth and Gaheris without even seeing who they were. Lancelot had loved Gareth like a younger brother. He had made Gareth a knight himself. Lancelot grabbed the queen, put her on a horse, and carried her away to his castle, Joyous Gard.",
    "When Gawaine heard that his brothers Gareth and Gaheris were dead, he fainted from grief. When he woke up, he swore vengeance. “I will never rest until Lancelot is dead,” he said. “King Arthur, if you are my uncle, you must make war on him.”",
    "And so a civil war began. Knights who had sat side by side at the Round Table now fought against one another. Arthur's army laid siege to Joyous Gard. Lancelot did not want to fight his king. When Arthur himself was knocked from his horse in battle, Lancelot would not let anyone harm him. He helped the king back onto his horse and said, “My lord, I will never strike you.” Arthur wept when he saw Lancelot's loyalty.",
    "At last, the Pope sent a message commanding King Arthur to take back the queen and make peace with Lancelot. Lancelot agreed. He brought Guenevere back to Arthur with great respect. “My lord,” he said, “I return your queen to you, and I swear she is true to you.”",
    "Arthur would have forgiven Lancelot. But Gawaine would not forgive. “I will never make peace with the man who killed my brothers,” he said. Because of Gawaine's anger, Lancelot was ordered to leave Britain forever. The fellowship of the Round Table, which had once seemed unbreakable, was now broken in two."])

ch("The Departure of Sir Lancelot",
   [("exile", "being forced to leave your home country"), ("mercy", "kindness shown to someone you could punish"),
    ("regent", "a person who rules while the king is away"), ("grudge", "a long-lasting feeling of anger toward someone")],
   ["Lancelot stood in the great hall of Camelot one last time. “I came to this court as a young man,” he said, “and here I won all my honor. Now I must leave in exile. I hope that God will bring peace between us someday.” Many people cried as he left. The queen cried most of all.",
    "Lancelot sailed to his own lands in France, and many knights who were loyal to him went with him. There he ruled wisely and gave land and castles to his friends.",
    "But Gawaine's grudge was still burning. He would not let King Arthur rest. At last, Arthur gathered an army and sailed to France to make war on Lancelot. Before he left, Arthur needed someone to rule Britain while he was gone. He chose his nephew, Sir Mordred, to be regent. Arthur did not know how dangerous this choice would be.",
    "Arthur's army laid siege to Lancelot's city of Benwick. Lancelot did not want to fight. He sent a message offering peace, but Gawaine refused. Instead, Gawaine rode up to the city walls every day and shouted insults, calling Lancelot a traitor and a coward.",
    "At last, Lancelot could not bear it any longer. He agreed to fight Gawaine. The two great knights met on the field. Gawaine had a strange gift: from morning until noon, his strength grew greater and greater, until he was three times as strong as usual. Lancelot knew this, so he defended himself carefully all morning, letting Gawaine wear himself out. After noon, Gawaine's strength returned to normal, and Lancelot struck him a great blow on the head. Gawaine fell to the ground.",
    "“Finish me!” Gawaine shouted. But Lancelot would not. “I will never strike a knight who is down,” he said. He showed mercy and walked away.",
    "When Gawaine's wound healed, he challenged Lancelot again, and once more Lancelot defeated him and spared his life. Gawaine's old wound opened, and he was badly hurt. Still, he would not give up his grudge.",
    "Then a messenger arrived from Britain with terrible news. Sir Mordred had betrayed King Arthur. He had told the people that Arthur had been killed in France, and he had made himself king. Now he was trying to force Queen Guenevere to marry him. The queen had escaped and locked herself in the Tower of London, and Mordred's army was surrounding it.",
    "King Arthur ended the war with Lancelot at once and sailed home with his army. Lancelot stayed in France, sorrowful that he could not help his king. The man Arthur had trusted to protect his kingdom had turned against him, and the knight who had always been Arthur's greatest defender was far across the sea."])

ch("The Last Battle and the Death of Arthur",
   [("treaty", "an agreement to stop fighting"), ("adder", "a poisonous snake"),
    ("mortal", "causing death"), ("Avalon", "a magical island where Arthur was carried to be healed")],
   ["When King Arthur's ships landed at Dover, Mordred's army was waiting on the beach. There was a fierce battle, and Arthur's army drove Mordred back. But Sir Gawaine, who was still weak from Lancelot's blow, was wounded again in the same place. He knew he was dying.",
    "Before he died, Gawaine finally let go of his anger. “This is my own fault,” he told Arthur. “If Lancelot were here, Mordred would never have dared to do this. My stubborn pride has brought all this trouble.” He wrote a letter to Lancelot, asking his forgiveness and begging him to come quickly and help the king. Then Gawaine died, and Arthur grieved for him.",
    "The night before the next battle, Arthur had a dream. Gawaine appeared to him and warned him. “Do not fight Mordred tomorrow,” Gawaine said. “If you do, you will be killed. Make a treaty and wait a month. Lancelot will come with his knights and save you.”",
    "So Arthur sent messengers to Mordred and agreed to meet him to make a treaty. Each leader would bring fourteen knights, and their armies would wait nearby. But neither side trusted the other. Arthur told his army, “If you see any sword drawn, attack.” Mordred told his army the same thing.",
    "The two leaders met on a field near a place called Camlann. They talked and agreed on peace. But as they were finishing, a small adder slithered out of a bush and bit one of the knights on the foot. Without thinking, the knight drew his sword to kill the snake.",
    "When the two armies saw the flash of a sword, each thought it was a signal of treachery. Trumpets blew, horns sounded, and the two armies charged. It was the most terrible battle that had ever been fought in Britain. It went on all day, until nearly everyone on both sides was dead.",
    "At the end of the day, Arthur looked around. Only two of his knights were left alive, Sir Lucan and his brother, Sir Bedivere. Then Arthur saw Mordred standing alone among the fallen. Arthur charged at him and struck him with his spear. With the last of his strength, Mordred swung his sword and gave the king a mortal wound. Then Mordred fell dead.",
    "Sir Bedivere and Sir Lucan carried the wounded king to a little chapel near the sea. Sir Lucan, who was badly hurt himself, died there. Arthur turned to Bedivere.",
    "“Take Excalibur,” he said, “and go to the water's edge. Throw it into the lake and come back and tell me what you see.”",
    "Bedivere took the sword, but when he looked at its jeweled handle, he could not bear to throw such a beautiful thing away. He hid it under a tree and went back. “What did you see?” asked the king. “Nothing but wind and waves,” Bedivere said. “You are lying to me,” said Arthur. “Go again.”",
    "Bedivere went back, but again he could not throw the sword, and again he lied. The third time, Arthur said, “You are a traitor to me if you do not obey.” This time, Bedivere ran to the water, whirled the sword around his head, and threw it as far as he could. A hand and an arm rose out of the water. It caught Excalibur by the handle, waved it three times, and pulled it down beneath the surface.",
    "Bedivere carried the king to the shore. A small boat floated there, carrying several queens dressed in black. One of them was Nimue, the Lady of the Lake. They lifted Arthur gently into the boat. “I am going to the island of Avalon to be healed of my wound,” Arthur told Bedivere. “If you never hear of me again, pray for my soul.” The boat floated away into the mist.",
    "Some people say that King Arthur died and was buried. Others say that he is not dead at all, but is sleeping in Avalon, and that one day, when Britain needs him most, he will return. Long afterward, it was said that these words were carved on his tomb: HERE LIES ARTHUR, THE ONCE AND FUTURE KING."])

ch("The Death of Guenevere and of Sir Lancelot",
   [("abbey", "a place where nuns or monks live and pray"), ("nun", "a woman who gives her life to prayer in a religious community"),
    ("peace", "calm; freedom from war and worry"), ("courtliest", "most polite and noble")],
   ["When Queen Guenevere heard that King Arthur and all his knights had died in the last battle, she was filled with sorrow. She left London and went to an abbey at Amesbury. There she became a nun. She wore plain black and white clothes and spent her days praying and helping the poor. Everyone who knew her was amazed at how humble and kind she became.",
    "In France, Lancelot received Gawaine's letter, and he hurried to Britain with his knights to help the king. But he came too late. When he heard that Arthur and Gawaine were dead and the kingdom was in ruins, he wept bitterly. He knelt at Gawaine's tomb and prayed for him for two nights.",
    "Then Lancelot went searching for the queen. At last he found her at the abbey in Amesbury. When Guenevere saw him, she fainted. When she woke, she spoke to him in front of all the nuns.",
    "“Because of the love between us,” she said, “the noblest knights in the world have been killed and my lord the king is dead. I have chosen to spend the rest of my life asking God's forgiveness. I ask you to go back to your own country and never come to see me again. Pray for me, and I will pray for you.”",
    "Lancelot answered sadly, “If this is the life you have chosen, then I will choose it too.” He kissed her goodbye and left. Neither of them ever saw the other alive again.",
    "Lancelot rode into the forest until he found a small chapel where a hermit lived. The hermit was Sir Bedivere, who had become a holy man after Arthur's death. Lancelot stayed there and became a priest. Some of his loyal knights joined him. For six years they lived simply, praying and fasting.",
    "One night, Lancelot had a dream telling him that Guenevere had died and that he should bring her body to be buried beside King Arthur. He and his companions walked to Amesbury and found that the queen had died just half an hour before they arrived. They carried her body to Glastonbury and buried her next to King Arthur's tomb. Lancelot was so full of sorrow that he could barely stand.",
    "After that, Lancelot would not eat or drink much. He grew weaker each day. Six weeks later, he died peacefully in his sleep. The hermit dreamed that he saw angels carrying Lancelot's soul up to heaven. His friends carried his body to Joyous Gard and buried him there, as he had wished.",
    "Lancelot's brother, Sir Ector de Maris, came to the chapel and saw his brother's body. He knelt and spoke the words that are still remembered today. “Ah, Lancelot,” he said, “you were the best of all knights. You were the most loyal friend, the truest lover, the kindest man who ever struck with a sword, the courtliest knight who ever sat among ladies, and the gentlest man in the hall, yet the sternest knight to your enemy who ever put a spear in its rest.”",
    "The surviving knights went to the Holy Land and spent the rest of their lives fighting for good causes. And so ended the days of King Arthur and the knights of the Round Table.",
    "The Round Table had been broken, and its knights were gone. But their story did not end. People told it again and again, for hundreds of years, in poems and plays and books like this one. The legend of a king who tried to build a kingdom of fairness and courage, and of knights who promised to protect the weak, has never been forgotten. Perhaps that is how King Arthur keeps his promise to return."])
